I have just bought a Sun server for running and compiling
a SPARC Linux kernel and distribution. I've tried to compile
a 32-bit kernel but I'm getting errors. I will also try to build
a 64-bit kernel when I get the 64-bit tool chain right.

I tend to compile my desktop kernels with only the necessary
modules built in and as many modules as possible. Usually
this works correctly on x86 but I have had errors doing the
same on MIPS, ARM and SPARC.

This could be caused by configurations that don't make sense
because the hardware is architecture specific. It would be
nice if those drivers that are known not to compile or to be
irrelevant for the architecture were disabled during the
configuration step so as to prevent failed compiles.

I know I could get a working config by disabling the drivers
that fail to compile but I'd rather have them either fixed
or disabled in the kernel config so as to prevent the same
thing in the future.

I have attached my error messages and kernel config.
I know it's for an older kernel release and I will try again
with the 2.6.22.1 and the upcoming 2.6.23 kernel versions.

Regards,
Sunil Janki

